The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (test_and_set_bit(map->req_idx, dmamux->used_chans)) {
    ret = -EBUSY;
    goto free_map;
}
```
```
clear_bitmap:
    clear_bit(map->req_idx, dmamux->used_chans);
```

Reason:
The `dma_spec->np` (an OF node) is obtained earlier via `of_parse_phandle()`. When `test_and_set_bit` fails (channel already in use), the code jumps directly to `free_map` without calling `of_node_put(dma_spec->np)`, causing an OF node reference leak. Similarly, after `clear_bitmap` (e.g., if the `r9a06g032_sysctrl_set_dmamux` call fails), the code falls through to `free_map` without releasing the OF node reference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+put_dma_spec_np:
+	of_node_put(dma_spec->np);
 free_map:
 	kfree(map);
```
```
if (test_and_set_bit(map->req_idx, dmamux->used_chans)) {
    ret = -EBUSY;
-    goto free_map;
+    goto put_dma_spec_np;
}
```
```
	clear_bit(map->req_idx, dmamux->used_chans);
+put_dma_spec_np:
+	of_node_put(dma_spec->np);
```

Reason:
The added `put_dma_spec_np` label calls `of_node_put(dma_spec->np)` before freeing the map and putting the device, ensuring the OF node reference is released in all error paths that occur after the node was parsed. The `EBUSY` case now jumps to this new label, and the `clear_bitmap` path falls through to it, eliminating the leak.

